# React Compiler Best Practices

This project uses the React Compiler (`babel-plugin-react-compiler`). The compiler automatically memoizes variables, functions, and component output. Follow these rules.

## Do not use memoization hooks

Never use `useMemo`, `useCallback`, or `React.memo` for performance. The compiler handles this.

```tsx
// BAD — redundant with the compiler
const items = useMemo(() => data.filter(isActive), [data]);
const handleClick = useCallback(() => select(id), [id, select]);

// GOOD — plain code, compiler memoizes automatically
const items = data.filter(isActive);
function handleClick() { select(id); }
```

## Hooks that are NOT memoization

Keep using `useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, `useReducer`, `useContext`, and custom hooks — these serve purposes the compiler does not replace.

## Keep render pure

The compiler assumes render functions are pure. Do not mutate external state or trigger side effects during render.

```tsx
// BAD — mutation during render
const list = [];
for (const item of items) { list.push(transform(item)); } // mutates list

// GOOD — build fresh value
const list = items.map(transform);
```

## Do not read refs during render

The compiler cannot track `.current` mutations. Reading a ref to decide what to render breaks compiler assumptions.

```tsx
// BAD — ref read determines render output
if (!targetRef.current) return null;

// GOOD — accept the value as a prop
function Gizmo({ target }: { target: Object3D }) { ... }
```

## Rules of Hooks still apply

Never call hooks conditionally or after early returns. The compiler does not relax this rule.
